* ATmega328P Pin Definitions -- 32-lead TQFP / 32-pad QFN/MLF package
* Source: Atmel-7810D "Automotive Microcontrollers ATmega328P Datasheet",
*         Figure 1-1 (Pinout) and Section 1.1 (Pin Descriptions).
*         Extracted directly from the vendor PDF's text layer
*         (permissions-encrypted against editing but not against text
*         extraction/copying).
*
* IMPORTANT -- this is the AUTOMOTIVE-grade datasheet specifically
* (Atmel-7810), which documents ONLY the 32-lead TQFP/QFN package. It
* does NOT cover the 28-pin PDIP/SOIC/MLF package that "ATmega328P" is
* commonly associated with in non-automotive contexts (e.g. the chip
* used on many hobbyist boards) -- that package is documented in a
* separate, non-automotive ATmega328P datasheet, not this one, and
* would need its own .qpindef file (genuinely different pin count,
* not just a package variant of this same 32-pin signal set -- the
* 28-pin package omits PC6 as a distinct pin arrangement, drops ADC6/
* ADC7 entirely, and renumbers everything). This file covers only what
* this specific (automotive) datasheet documents.
*
* Roughly half of this device's 32 physical pins (9-16 and 25-32) sit
* in the pinout diagram's top/bottom-edge regions, where label and
* number columns are separated by the same text-extraction scrambling
* seen on other devices in this project -- no reliable positional-
* inference method was found for those two regions. Per this project's
* established principle (pin numbers are not part of the .qpindef
* format and do not block inclusion of a signal whose NAME is
* otherwise confirmed), every signal below was still confirmed to
* exist via direct, clear reading of its label text -- only the
* specific physical pin number for those 16 pins is unresolved. Every
* pin's ALT-TEXT was read directly from that same diagram, though,
* since (unlike PIC24/D21/E54) this device's pin diagram carries the
* full alternate-function text inline with each pin name -- no
* separate multiplexing table was needed.
*
* Flagged for manual review (dual-function / non-obvious pins):
*   PC6/RESET -- classic fuse-selectable dual-function pin. Default
*              (RSTDISBL fuse unprogrammed) is dedicated active-low
*              Reset input. If RSTDISBL is programmed, PC6 becomes a
*              GPIO pin -- but the datasheet explicitly states it is
*              "used as an input pin" in that mode, i.e. input-only
*              even when GPIO-enabled, never a general bidirectional
*              pin. Classified I to reflect that -- not B, despite
*              PORTC otherwise being bidirectional.
*   PB6, PB7 -- oscillator-mode-dependent, on top of their normal GPIO
*              function. Depending on the clock-selection fuse
*              settings: PB6 can serve as input to the inverting
*              oscillator amplifier (XTAL1) and PB7 as its output
*              (XTAL2); separately, if the internal calibrated RC
*              oscillator is used as the chip clock source, this same
*              pin pair can instead serve as TOSC1/TOSC2 for the
*              asynchronous Timer/Counter2. Classified B (matches
*              "Port B is bidirectional") for their default GPIO role,
*              but availability as GPIO depends on which of these
*              fuse-selected modes is active.
*   ADC6, ADC7 -- genuinely dedicated analog-input-only pins with NO
*              GPIO alternate at all, unlike ADC0-ADC5 (which share
*              PC0-PC5). Datasheet: "In the TQFP and QFN/MLF package,
*              ADC7:6 serve as analog inputs to the A/D converter."
*              Package-dependent per that same sentence, though this
*              file only covers the TQFP/QFN/MLF package, where they
*              are always present.
*   AVCC     -- separate ANALOG power rail. Datasheet explicitly notes
*              "It should be externally connected to VCC, even if the
*              ADC is not used" -- same nuance as AVDD-style pins on
*              other devices in this project (same voltage in
*              practice, architecturally separate pin/rail). Recorded
*              as I per this project's established workaround.
*   AREF     -- external ADC voltage reference input; dedicated, not
*              multiplexed with any GPIO port bit.
*
* VCC occupies two physical pins (4 and 6); GND occupies three (3, 5,
* and 21). Same net regardless of physical pin count in both cases;
* one V and one G record cover them.
*
* No companion devices -- this datasheet covers only ATmega328P, no
* plain ATmega328 or other memory-size variant.

D Atmel ATmega328P 32-Pin Automotive-Grade 8-bit AVR Microcontroller with 32K Bytes In-System Programmable Flash
P ATmega328P

V VCC
I ADC6
I ADC7
I AREF
I AVCC
B PB0 PCINT0/CLKO/ICP1
B PB1 PCINT1/OC1A
B PB2 PCINT2/SS/OC1B
B PB3 PCINT3/OC2A/MOSI
B PB4 PCINT4/MISO
B PB5 SCK/PCINT5
B PB6 PCINT6/XTAL1/TOSC1
B PB7 PCINT7/XTAL2/TOSC2
B PC0 ADC0/PCINT8
K SIMCLK

B PC1 ADC1/PCINT9
B PC2 ADC2/PCINT10
B PC3 ADC3/PCINT11
B PC4 ADC4/SDA/PCINT12
B PC5 ADC5/SCL/PCINT13
I PC6 RESET/PCINT14
B PD0 RXD/PCINT16
B PD1 TXD/PCINT17
B PD2 INT0/PCINT18
B PD3 PCINT19/OC2B/INT1
B PD4 PCINT20/XCK/T0
B PD5 PCINT21/OC0B/T1
B PD6 PCINT22/OC0A/AIN0
B PD7 PCINT23/AIN1
G GND
